        {
          "slug": "bounce",
          "name": "Bounce",
          "aliases": ["down bounce", "up bounce", "pulse"],
          "difficulty": "Beginner",
          "summary": "Knees giving and returning on every beat — the pulse under everything.",
          "description": "The bounce is the simplest groove in house and the one that never switches off. In a down bounce the knees bend on the beat; in an up bounce you rise on the beat and sink on the \"and\". It is small and elastic, driven from the knees and ankles, and it is what every step on this page is layered on top of. Before the jack, before any footwork, the bounce is how you show you are hearing the kick drum.",
          "steps": [
            "Stand with feet hip-width apart, weight on the balls of the feet, knees soft.",
            "On each beat, let the knees give a few centimetres and spring back — a down bounce.",
            "Keep the upper body relaxed and let it ride the bounce rather than holding it still.",
            "Switch to an up bounce: rise onto the beat, sink on the \"and\" between beats.",
            "Hold either bounce for a full track without it drifting off the kick."
          ],
          "tips": [
            "Small is better. A big bounce is tiring and pulls everything else off time.",
            "If you lose the beat mid-step, drop back to just the bounce until you find it again."
          ],
          "related": ["jack", "one-leg-bounce", "on-the-one"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "jack",
          "name": "The jack",
          "aliases": ["front jack", "forward jack", "jacking"],
          "difficulty": "Beginner",
          "danceSlug": "house-jack",
          "summary": "The core torso wave — chest and pelvis rocking in opposition on every beat.",
          "description": "The jack is the signature groove of house. The chest and the pelvis move in opposition: as the chest drops back and down, the hips tip forward and the knees bend; as the chest rolls forward and up, the knees straighten. Done continuously it looks like a wave running through the body on every kick. Dancers accent it both ways — contracting on the beat or releasing on the beat — and switch between them to change the feel of the same step.",
          "steps": [
            "Start in a relaxed bounce: feet hip-width, knees soft, weight forward on the balls of the feet.",
            "On the beat, let the chest sink back and down as the knees bend; the pelvis tips forward.",
            "On the \"and\", release: the chest rolls forward and up while the knees straighten slightly.",
            "Keep it continuous — no pause at either end — so the motion rolls chest to hips like a wave.",
            "Let the head and arms hang loose and ride on top; do not drive the move with a nod.",
            "Flip the accent so the chest hits forward on the beat instead, and practise switching between the two."
          ],
          "tips": [
            "Learn it at half-time first — one jack every two beats — then double it.",
            "The most common mistake is nodding the head and calling it a jack. The movement lives in the chest and the knees.",
            "Keep it going under every footwork step you learn next. That is the whole point of it."
          ],
          "related": ["side-jack", "rolling-jack", "bounce", "jacking"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "side-jack",
          "name": "Side jack",
          "aliases": ["lateral jack"],
          "difficulty": "Beginner",
          "summary": "The jack turned sideways — ribcage and hips swaying across the body on the beat.",
          "description": "The side jack takes the same opposition as the front jack and turns it ninety degrees. The ribcage shifts to one side while the hips answer to the other, and the knees keep bouncing underneath. It pairs naturally with footwork that travels sideways, like the skate and the sidewalk, and switching between the front and side jack is the first step towards the rolling jack.",
          "steps": [
            "Start in a bounce with feet a little wider than hip-width.",
            "On the beat, slide the ribcage to the right while the hips shift slightly left; the knees bend.",
            "On the next beat, send the ribcage left and the hips right.",
            "Keep the shoulders level — the ribcage slides, it does not tilt.",
            "Alternate front jack and side jack every eight counts to practise switching."
          ],
          "tips": [
            "Isolate the ribcage in front of a mirror first; many people tilt the shoulders instead of sliding.",
            "Let the arms swing with the ribcage rather than holding them away from the body."
          ],
          "related": ["jack", "rolling-jack", "skate", "sidewalk"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "rolling-jack",
          "name": "Rolling jack",
          "aliases": ["circle jack", "circular jack"],
          "difficulty": "Intermediate",
          "summary": "Front and side jacks joined into a continuous circle of the torso.",
          "description": "The rolling jack links the directions of the jack into one circle: forward, to the side, back, to the other side. It usually takes two or four beats to complete a circle, and it can run in either direction. It is less about hitting each beat than about a smooth, unbroken roll — a good way to fill space in a groove or ride a long melodic section of a track.",
          "steps": [
            "Start with a front jack to find the timing.",
            "Push the chest forward, then carry it round to the right side.",
            "Continue the chest back, then round to the left side, and forward again — one full circle.",
            "Let the hips circle in opposition to the chest, knees bending as the chest passes back.",
            "Reverse the direction of the circle, then practise changing direction mid-phrase."
          ],
          "tips": [
            "Keep the circle even — people often rush the back half.",
            "Start with a small circle. A bigger one looks good only when it stays smooth."
          ],
          "related": ["jack", "side-jack"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "one-leg-bounce",
          "name": "Bounce on one leg",
          "aliases": ["one-leg bounce", "single-leg bounce"],
          "difficulty": "Beginner",
          "danceSlug": "bounce-one-leg",
          "summary": "Keeping the pulse going with all your weight on one side.",
          "description": "Most footwork needs one foot free while the other supports you, and the groove has to survive that. The one-leg bounce is the bounce or jack done entirely on the standing leg, with the free foot lifted or resting lightly on the floor. It builds the balance and leg strength that knee-ups, kicks and the farmer all depend on.",
          "steps": [
            "Start in a bounce, then shift all your weight onto the left leg.",
            "Lift the right foot slightly off the floor, knee relaxed.",
            "Keep bouncing on the left leg on every beat without the upper body bracing.",
            "Add a jack on top once the balance is steady.",
            "Switch legs every eight counts."
          ],
          "tips": [
            "Keep the standing knee soft — a straight leg kills the bounce.",
            "Find a point to look at; balance is much harder with the eyes on the floor."
          ],
          "related": ["bounce", "knee-up", "farmer"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "stomp",
          "name": "Stomp",
          "aliases": ["stomping"],
          "difficulty": "Beginner",
          "summary": "A flat, heavy step into the floor that makes the kick drum visible.",
          "description": "The stomp is the most direct way to dance the kick: a flat-footed step driven down into the floor on the beat, with the jack sinking into it. It is used alone as a groove, to punctuate the end of a phrase, and inside other steps where a hard accent is wanted. The contrast between stomps and light footwork is one of the easiest ways to add dynamics to a freestyle.",
          "steps": [
            "Start in a jack with your weight centred.",
            "Lift the right foot a few centimetres and put it down flat and firm on the beat, sinking the knees.",
            "Repeat with the left foot on the next beat.",
            "Try stomping only on beats 1 and 3, and bouncing lightly on 2 and 4."
          ],
          "tips": [
            "The weight goes down through the leg, not just the foot slapping the floor — the sound is a by-product.",
            "Be kind to your neighbours and your joints: sink into the stomp rather than locking the knee on impact."
          ],
          "related": ["jack", "on-the-one"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "jack-in-the-box",
          "name": "Jack in the box",
          "aliases": ["box step", "jazz box with a jack"],
          "difficulty": "Intermediate",
          "summary": "A four-step box pattern with a jack on every step.",
          "description": "Jack in the box puts the jack onto a square floor pattern taken from the jazz box: the feet step across, back, out and together, returning to where they started after four counts. Every step lands with a jack, so the pattern tests whether the groove survives the feet moving in four directions. Turning the box a quarter each time is a common variation.",
          "steps": [
            "Start in a jack, feet together.",
            "Count 1: cross the right foot over in front of the left, jacking into it.",
            "Count 2: step the left foot back.",
            "Count 3: step the right foot out to the side.",
            "Count 4: bring the left foot forward to close the box, then repeat.",
            "Once it is smooth, add a quarter turn on each repeat."
          ],
          "tips": [
            "The torso keeps jacking forward even when the feet go backwards — that disconnection is the skill.",
            "Keep the box small. A wide box drags you off the beat."
          ],
          "related": ["jack", "crossroads", "pas-de-bourree"]
        }
