        {
          "slug": "heel-toe",
          "name": "Heel-toe",
          "aliases": ["heel toe"],
          "difficulty": "Beginner",
          "summary": "Tap the heel, then the toe, alternating sides over a bounce.",
          "description": "Heel-toe is one of the first footwork patterns most house dancers learn. The free foot taps its heel out in front, then its toe (either in the same place or back beside the standing foot), before the feet change over. It trains weight changes on the \"and\", which almost every faster step needs, and it has many variations — crossing the heel over, tapping the toe behind, or swivelling heel and toe to travel sideways.",
          "steps": [
            "Start in a bounce, weight on the left foot.",
            "On 1, tap the right heel forward on the floor, toes lifted.",
            "On \"and\", bring the right foot back and tap its toe beside the left foot.",
            "On 2, change weight onto the right foot and tap the left heel forward.",
            "Keep alternating, then try crossing the heel slightly in front of the standing foot."
          ],
          "tips": [
            "The weight change is the hard part — make sure you are genuinely standing on the other foot before the next heel.",
            "Keep the taps light; the standing leg carries the bounce."
